WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:06.259
Film Courage: So your latest film CREATED
EQUAL, how many people would show up [on set]

00:00:06.259 --> 00:00:07.259
everyday?

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:15.610
Bill Duke, Actor and Director: Depending on
the day, the courthouse scenes, lots and lots.

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:25.650
Similar scenes, if you’re directing, you’re
in charge of a whole lot of folks (30, 40,

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:27.279
50 sometimes 100 more).

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:29.171
A big feature film, a few hundred.

00:00:29.171 --> 00:00:41.270
And you have to translate your vision to everybody
from The Teamsters to the actors to the first

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:57.620
and second AD, the DP, sound, wardrobe, props,
costume because for me I have…I’m a very

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:03.480
paranoid director.

00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:10.800
I go through my sets at least 5 to 7 times
(every set).

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:18.260
I block, my DP, everybody comes with me and
I have my assistant write my shot list.

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:27.640
So every angle, every shot, every set-up my
entire staff and crew have it a week before

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.350
I shoot.

00:01:29.350 --> 00:01:38.390
There’s no drama, no mystery, you come to
the set, cast comes in, I know the blocking

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:45.241
(I may make some adjustments) but they know
everything so that when we’re there, we

00:01:45.241 --> 00:01:46.241
work.

00:01:46.241 --> 00:01:49.280
It’s not figuring things out.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.979
Rehearsals give you room to…for the actors
who don’t feel comfortable sitting here

00:01:53.979 --> 00:01:56.060
“Can I sit over here?”

00:01:56.060 --> 00:01:58.159
Fine, that’s not a problem.

00:01:58.159 --> 00:02:02.760
But we don’t say “Wait a minute?

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:06.640
Instead of waiting for the sun to shine, let’s
wait for the sun to set.”

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.399
That don’t happen.

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:12.240
Because that means you didn’t make your
day.

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.770
You just have to be planning.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:21.110
Directors, they’re in charge of two things.

00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:26.180
Creative process - you have to translate your
vision to everybody.

00:02:26.180 --> 00:02:29.090
But the second thing is - they are managers.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:38.010
They are managers of three things - time,
people, and money.

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:42.260
It’s mathematics.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:49.940
You have 12 hours in a day that you can work,
a certain number of scenes, that are broken

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:52.450
down into set-ups.

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:56.790
Each set-up you have a certain number of minutes
to make your day.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:05.880
An actor comes in unprepared and um…that’s
a big problem.

00:03:05.880 --> 00:03:11.579
So we work as a team, a collaborate effort,
understanding our obligations and responsibilities.

